---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Doc from '../components/legal/Doc.astro';
const updated = '29 October 2026';
// Drafts stay out of search until counsel signs off and every placeholder is filled (design/legal/README.md).
const draft = true;
---

<Base title="Brand agreement · Comarket" description="The agreement for brands on Comarket: fees per completed sale, the new-customer rule, refunds, billing, creators, brand assets, data and liability." stage="none" noindex={draft}>
  <Doc
    title="Brand agreement"
    lede="You pay only for completed sales from new customers. This is the agreement behind that, in full."
    updated={updated}
    draft={draft}
    current="/brand-terms"
  >
    <div class="short">
      <b>The short version</b>
      <ul>
        <li>Nothing upfront and nothing monthly. For each completed sale you pay the creator rate you set, plus our fee: 15% of the new customer's first payment and 10% of their renewals, for 12 months. After that, the customer is all yours.</li>
        <li>A sale counts only if the buyer is new to you. We check their email, phone and card against your Stripe customers.</li>
        <li>A refund inside your refund window cancels the fee. Lost chargebacks and fraud are reversed too.</li>
        <li>You approve every creator before their storefront goes live, and you can end this agreement on 30 days' notice.</li>
        <li>We bill monthly and pay the creators ourselves, after your refund window closes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>1. About this agreement</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.1</span>This Brand Agreement is between [[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]] ("Comarket", "we", "us") and the business
      that accepts it when opening a brand account ("you"). The person accepting confirms that they can bind that business.
      It applies from the moment you accept it and covers your use of Comarket as a brand.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.2</span>It includes the general terms in <a href="/terms#part-b-general-terms">Part B of the Terms of
      use</a>, the <a href="/acceptable-use">acceptable use policy</a> and the <a href="/dpa">data processing addendum</a> (the
      "DPA"). If they conflict, this order applies: the DPA (for personal data), then any Offer terms you and a creator agree
      through Comarket (for that Offer only, and only where this agreement allows), then this agreement, then the
      acceptable use policy, then Part B.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.3</span>Comarket is for businesses. You confirm that you are acting for your trade or business, not as a
      consumer.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Words with a set meaning</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Creator:</b> a person or business that has accepted the <a href="/creator-terms">Creator Agreement</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Offer:</b> what you ask creators to promote: the product, any price, trial or discount for buyers, the Creator Rate, the Refund Window, the countries where it may be promoted, and the claims creators may make about it.</li>
      <li><b>Approved Creator:</b> a Creator you have approved for an Offer.</li>
      <li><b>Storefront:</b> the page we host for an Approved Creator and your Offer, in your look and with the creator's name on it, at an address such as yourbrand.comarket.si/creators/name.</li>
      <li><b>Hub:</b> a Creator's page at comarket.si/their-handle, which links to their Storefronts.</li>
      <li><b>Connected Stripe Account:</b> each Stripe account you connect to Comarket, through which buyers pay you.</li>
      <li><b>Attributed Checkout:</b> a checkout in your Connected Stripe Account that a buyer starts from a Storefront and that carries that Storefront's reference.</li>
      <li><b>Customer Records:</b> the customer, payment, subscription, refund and dispute records in your Connected Stripe Account, and any exclusion list you give us under 6.4.</li>
      <li><b>First Payment:</b> the first successful payment of more than zero that a New Customer makes from an Attributed Checkout, including the first payment after a free trial that started there.</li>
      <li><b>Renewal:</b> each later successful payment by the same customer, through your Connected Stripe Account, for the product in the Offer or a plan, upgrade or add-on of it, made during the Commission Period.</li>
      <li><b>Commission Period:</b> the 12 months starting on the date of the First Payment.</li>
      <li><b>Net Amount:</b> what the buyer actually paid for a payment, after discounts and credits, excluding VAT, GST, sales tax and similar taxes you collect, and less any part refunded. Card and Stripe processing fees are not deducted.</li>
      <li><b>Creator Rate:</b> what you agree to pay the creator, set in the Offer either as a percentage of the Net Amount of each First Payment and Renewal, or as a fixed amount per Qualifying Sale.</li>
      <li><b>Comarket Fee:</b> our fee, set out in section 7.</li>
      <li><b>Refund Window:</b> the period after each payment, set in the Offer, during which a buyer may get a refund. It may not exceed [[MAXIMUM REFUND WINDOW]] days. If an Offer doesn't set one, it is 14 days.</li>
      <li><b>Qualifying Sale:</b> a sale that meets section 6.</li>
      <li><b>Statement:</b> the monthly record described in section 9.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. What Comarket does</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.1</span>We find Creators whose audiences fit your Offer, introduce them to you, build and host a
      Storefront for each Approved Creator, link each Storefront to your checkout, decide which sales count, bill you, and pay
      the Creators.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.2</span>You sell to buyers, not us. You are the seller of record for every sale, and your own terms of
      sale, refund policy and privacy policy apply to buyers. We are not your agent for selling, and we don't take or handle
      buyers' payments; buyers pay you through your Connected Stripe Account.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.3</span><b>How we match.</b> We suggest Creators to you, and Offers to Creators, mainly on: how closely
      their topic and audience fit the problem your product solves; where their audience is; their platforms and formats;
      their past results on Comarket, such as completed sales and refund rates; whether their accepted rates fit yours; any
      preferences you set; and our checks for quality, compliance and fraud. No one can pay for a higher place in matching.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.4</span>We don't promise any number of creators, posts, clicks or sales. Creators are independent: they
      decide what to post and when, within the Offer, the Creator Agreement and the acceptable use policy.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Setting up</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.1</span><b>Stripe.</b> You connect your Stripe account through Stripe's own authorisation screen. You
      give us read access to your Customer Records and the ability to receive Stripe event notifications about them, so we can
      run the checks in section 6 and calculate fees. We don't move money in your Stripe account or change your products,
      prices or customers. Keep the account connected, and don't remove the Storefront reference from Attributed Checkouts,
      while this agreement lasts and until the last Commission Period ends.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.2</span><b>Offers.</b> You set each Offer and are responsible for it. You can change or end an Offer
      going forward. A change to the Creator Rate or the Refund Window applies only to Attributed Checkouts started after
      creators are told of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.3</span><b>Storefronts.</b> We build each Storefront from your Brand Assets (section 14), the Offer and the
      Approved Creator's profile. You approve the Storefront design before any Storefront for that Offer goes live. Every
      Storefront shows a notice that the creator earns a commission; you may not ask us to remove it.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Approving creators</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.1</span>No Storefront goes live until you approve the creator. You may decline any creator, without
      giving a reason, as long as the decision is not based on a characteristic protected by law.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.2</span>You may withdraw an approval at any time. We take the Storefront down within 2 business days.
      Attributed Checkouts started before the Storefront came down still count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.3</span>Creators may decline your Offer, or stop promoting it, at any time.
    </p>

    <h2>6. What counts as a sale</h2>
    <p><span class="n">6.1</span>A sale is a Qualifying Sale when all of these are true:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>the buyer started an Attributed Checkout from an Approved Creator's Storefront;</li>
      <li>the First Payment succeeded within [[ATTRIBUTION WINDOW]] days after the buyer last opened that Storefront;</li>
      <li>the buyer is a New Customer under 6.2;</li>
      <li>the First Payment was not fully refunded within its Refund Window; and</li>
      <li>the sale is not excluded under 6.6.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.2</span><b>New Customer.</b> A buyer is a New Customer unless their email address, phone number or card
      fingerprint (a code Stripe derives from a card, never the card number) matches a customer in your Customer Records that
      existed before the Attributed Checkout began, or an entry on your exclusion list. A match on any one of the three makes
      the buyer an existing customer, and the sale doesn't count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.3</span>If none of the three identifiers is available for a buyer, the sale counts as new unless you show,
      under section 11, that the buyer was already your customer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.4</span><b>Exclusion list.</b> If you have customers outside your Connected Stripe Account, you may give
      us a list of their email addresses or phone numbers in the format we specify. A list applies to Attributed Checkouts
      started after we confirm we have loaded it. The DPA covers it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.5</span><b>One creator per sale.</b> The Storefront that started the Attributed Checkout gets the credit.
      We don't split a sale between creators, and a sale through a Storefront never also counts for another channel through
      Comarket.
    </p>
    <p><span class="n">6.6</span><b>Excluded sales.</b> A sale doesn't count, or stops counting, if:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the buyer is the creator, someone in their household, or someone acting for them;</li>
      <li>it came from conduct the Creator Agreement or the acceptable use policy forbids, such as cookie stuffing, paying buyers, or spam;</li>
      <li>it is a test, or a fraudulent or unauthorised payment; or</li>
      <li>the buyer is your employee or contractor and you told us so before the payment.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.7</span>Free trials don't count until the First Payment. Payments by a customer after their Commission
      Period ends never count.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Fees</h2>
    <p><span class="n">7.1</span>For each Qualifying Sale you pay:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>the Creator Rate,</b> on the First Payment and on each Renewal, unless the Offer limits it to the First Payment; and</li>
      <li><b>the Comarket Fee:</b> 15% of the Net Amount of the First Payment, and 10% of the Net Amount of each Renewal.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.2</span>Both stop when the Commission Period ends. Nothing is payable for that customer after that.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.3</span>There is no setup fee, subscription, minimum spend or fee for sales that don't count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.4</span><b>Rates you were shown.</b> If you joined the waitlist and were shown lower fee rates than these,
      section 3.4 of the <a href="/terms">Terms of use</a> applies and your Statement shows the rates used.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.5</span><b>Changes.</b> We may change the Comarket Fee by giving you at least 30 days' notice. A change
      applies only to Attributed Checkouts started after it takes effect. Customers already in a Commission Period keep the
      rates that applied when their Attributed Checkout started.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.6</span><b>Currency.</b> Fees are calculated in the currency of each payment and invoiced in
      [[BILLING CURRENCY]], converted at the rate Stripe applies on the invoice date.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.7</span><b>Taxes.</b> Fees exclude VAT and similar taxes, which we add where the law requires. Where the
      reverse charge applies, you account for the tax. If the law requires you to withhold tax from a payment to us, you will
      pay us an extra amount so that we receive what we would have received without the withholding, and give us the
      withholding certificate.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Refunds, chargebacks and reversals</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.1</span><b>Inside the Refund Window.</b> If a payment is fully refunded within its Refund Window, no Creator
      Rate or Comarket Fee is due on it. If it is partly refunded, both are reduced in proportion. Because we bill only for
      payments whose Refund Window has closed (section 9), you normally never pay for these.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.2</span><b>Chargebacks.</b> If a buyer disputes a payment with their bank within [[CHARGEBACK PERIOD]] days
      of the payment and you lose the dispute, the Creator Rate and Comarket Fee on that payment are reversed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.3</span><b>Fraud.</b> If a sale is excluded under 6.6, its fees are reversed whenever we find out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.4</span><b>Refunds after the window.</b> A refund made after the Refund Window closes doesn't reverse fees,
      except under 8.2 or 8.3.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.5</span><b>How reversals are paid.</b> We credit reversals on your next invoice. If there is no next
      invoice, we refund the credit within 30 days of the end of this agreement.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.6</span>Don't refund and re-charge a buyer, or move them to another account or payment route, to avoid
      fees on a Qualifying Sale. Fees remain due on any such sale.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Statements, invoices and payment</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.1</span>In the first 5 business days of each month, we issue a Statement and an invoice for every
      payment on a Qualifying Sale whose Refund Window closed in the previous month. The Statement lists each payment by its
      Stripe payment ID, which identifies the buyer only to you, with its date, Net Amount, the creator credited, the Creator
      Rate, the Comarket Fee and any reversals.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.2</span>We charge the card or bank account you keep on file through Stripe on the invoice date. If we
      agree to invoice you instead, payment is due within [[PAYMENT TERMS DAYS]] days.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.3</span>If an undisputed amount is unpaid 14 days after it is due and we have reminded you, we may charge
      interest at the rate allowed for late commercial payments under the governing law, claim reasonable recovery costs as
      that law allows, and pause your Storefronts until it is paid. Sales from Attributed Checkouts started before the pause
      still count.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Creator earnings</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.1</span>Each Creator appoints us as their limited agent to receive the Creator Rate from you. When you
      pay us an amount for a Creator Rate, your obligation to the creator for that amount is fully settled, whether or not we
      have paid the creator yet. You must not pay creators directly for Qualifying Sales.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">10.2</span>We pay creators under the Creator Agreement. We are not responsible to you for a creator's posts,
      conduct or tax affairs, but we enforce the Creator Agreement and the acceptable use policy, and we will act on any breach
      you report.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Questioning a sale</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.1</span>You may dispute any line on a Statement within [[SALE DISPUTE PERIOD]] days of receiving it, by
      telling us which line and why, for example that the buyer was already your customer. Pay the undisputed part on time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.2</span>A person at Comarket reviews each dispute, may ask you and the creator for information, and tells
      you the decision and the reasons within 30 days. If we agree, we credit the amount on your next invoice. If you still
      disagree, sections 16 and 17 of Part B apply.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">11.3</span>Lines you don't dispute in time are final, except for fraud or an obvious error.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Your responsibilities</h2>
    <p><span class="n">12.1</span>You are responsible for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>your products and services, their safety, delivery and support, your prices, your checkout, and your terms of sale and refund policy;</li>
      <li>the consumer, tax, product, licensing and sector rules that apply to selling them, in each country where you allow the Offer to be promoted;</li>
      <li>telling buyers, in your privacy policy, that you share purchase data with service providers like us to check new customers and credit referrals;</li>
      <li>making sure every claim in the Offer, and every claim you let creators make, is true and backed by evidence you can show; and</li>
      <li>the accuracy of what you tell us, including your exclusion list.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><span class="n">12.2</span>You must not:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>ask or encourage a creator to hide or weaken their disclosure, or to make a claim you haven't approved;</li>
      <li>interfere with Storefronts, references or our checks;</li>
      <li>offer products or use Comarket in a way the acceptable use policy forbids; or</li>
      <li>use what you learn about creators through Comarket for anything other than working with them on Comarket.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>13. Advertising law and disclosures</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">13.1</span>Storefronts and creators' posts about your Offer are advertising for you. Under the US Federal Trade
      Commission's Guides Concerning the Use of Endorsements and Testimonials in Advertising (16 CFR Part 255), the UK CAP Code
      and consumer protection law, the EU Unfair Commercial Practices Directive and the national laws that implement it,
      advertisers can be responsible for misleading endorsements and missing disclosures.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">13.2</span>Every Storefront discloses the creator's commission, and the Creator Agreement requires creators to
      disclose their connection to you clearly in every post. If you see a post or Storefront that breaks these rules, tell us
      at [[CONTACT EMAIL]]. We will act on it, and we may pause a Storefront while we do.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">13.3</span>We don't give legal advice about your advertising. Our Storefront design and disclosure wording
      don't make us responsible for the claims in your Offer.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Brand assets and content</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.1</span><b>Your licence to us.</b> "Brand Assets" means your names, logos, trademarks, colors, typefaces,
      product images, copy and other material you give us or publish on your website, including what our preview reads from
      your homepage. You give Comarket a non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free licence, for as long as this agreement lasts and
      the wind-down in 20.4, to use, copy, adapt for layout (such as resizing or cropping) and display Brand Assets to build,
      host and show Storefronts, Hubs, previews and our dashboards, and to let Approved Creators use them to promote your Offer
      under the Offer's rules. We may sublicense these rights only to Approved Creators and to our service providers.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.2</span>You confirm that you have the rights to give this licence, including to use your typefaces on the
      web. If you don't have web rights for a typeface, tell us and we will use a similar one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.3</span><b>Naming you as a customer.</b> We may name you and show your logo as a Comarket brand only with
      your consent, which you can withdraw at any time going forward.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.4</span><b>Ownership.</b> You keep all rights in your Brand Assets. We keep all rights in Comarket, its
      software, Storefront templates and designs, and the data we create in running the service (other than your Customer
      Records). Creators keep the rights in their content. You may display creators' content on your Storefronts only, and
      use it anywhere else (for example in your own ads) only if the creator has agreed to that through Comarket.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">14.5</span><b>Usage data.</b> We may use information about how Comarket performs, combined with information
      from other brands so that neither you nor any person can be identified, to run and improve Comarket and to publish
      general benchmarks.
    </p>

    <h2>15. Data protection</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">15.1</span>For the Customer Records and buyer data we process to run the new-customer check, credit sales and
      prepare your Statements, you are the controller and we are your processor (in California terms, your service provider).
      The <a href="/dpa">DPA</a> governs that processing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">15.2</span>We are an independent controller for: information about the people who use your account; our
      records of each Qualifying Sale (a reference, dates, amounts, the creator credited and the result of the checks), which
      we keep to bill you, pay creators, meet tax and accounting law, prevent fraud and handle disputes; and data about how
      Storefronts are used, which we keep to run and secure Comarket. Our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> describes this.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">15.3</span>We never give creators the identity or contact details of your buyers.
    </p>

    <h2>16. Promises each side makes</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">16.1</span>Each party promises that it may enter into this agreement and that it will comply with the laws
      that apply to its part in it, including anti-bribery and sanctions laws.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">16.2</span>We will provide Comarket with reasonable skill and care. Otherwise, to the extent the law allows,
      Comarket is provided "as is", and we give no other warranties, express or implied. We don't promise that Comarket will be
      uninterrupted or error-free, or that it will produce any level of sales.
    </p>

    <h2>17. Indemnities</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">17.1</span><b>From you.</b> You will defend Comarket against any claim by a third party (including a buyer,
      creator or regulator) to the extent it arises from your products or services, your Offers or the claims in them, your
      Brand Assets infringing someone's rights, your dealings with buyers, or your breach of consumer, advertising or data
      protection law. You will pay the damages, fines and reasonable costs finally awarded or agreed in settlement.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">17.2</span><b>From us.</b> We will defend you against any claim by a third party that Comarket itself, as we
      provide it, infringes their intellectual property rights, and pay the damages and reasonable costs finally awarded or
      agreed in settlement. This doesn't cover your Brand Assets, creators' content, third-party services, or anything changed
      or combined with something we didn't provide. If such a claim is made, we may change Comarket so it no longer infringes or,
      if that isn't reasonable, end the affected part and refund any fees paid for it in advance.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">17.3</span><b>How.</b> The party asking for defence must tell the other promptly, let it control the defence
      and settlement, and help at its cost. No settlement may admit fault for, or impose a duty on, the defended party without
      its consent.
    </p>

    <h2>18. Liability</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">18.1</span>Neither party is liable for lost profits, revenue, business or goodwill, or for indirect or
      consequential loss, arising from this agreement.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">18.2</span>Each party's total liability arising from this agreement in any 12-month period is limited to the
      greater of the Comarket Fees paid and payable by you in the 12 months before the event giving rise to the claim, and
      [[BRAND LIABILITY FLOOR]].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">18.3</span>Liability for breach of the DPA or of data protection law is limited separately to
      [[DATA PROTECTION CAP]] in total.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">18.4</span>No limit in this section applies to: your duty to pay Creator Rates and Comarket Fees; amounts
      under section 17; breach of confidentiality; fraud, gross negligence or wilful misconduct; death or personal injury caused
      by negligence; or any liability that can't be limited by law.
    </p>

    <h2>19. Suspension</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">19.1</span>We may pause an Offer, a Storefront or your account where we reasonably believe it is needed to
      stop a serious breach of this agreement or the acceptable use policy, fraud, a security risk, or a breach of law, or
      because a regulator or court requires it. We will tell you, with our reasons, when the suspension starts, unless the law
      or the risk of fraud prevents that.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">19.2</span>You can respond through the complaints process in Part B. We lift a suspension as soon as its
      reason is resolved.
    </p>

    <h2>20. Term and ending</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">20.1</span>This agreement lasts until either party ends it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">20.2</span>You may end it at any time by giving 30 days' notice. You may pause your Offers immediately at
      any time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">20.3</span>We may end it by giving 30 days' notice, with our reasons. We may end it sooner, with our reasons:
      if you materially breach it and don't fix the breach within 15 days of notice; if you don't pay an undisputed amount
      within 30 days of a reminder; for fraud or repeated breaches of the acceptable use policy; if you become insolvent; or if
      the law or a regulator requires us to.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">20.4</span><b>What happens at the end.</b> We take your Storefronts down within 10 business days and stop
      using your Brand Assets, except in records we must keep and in creators' posts already published, which creators don't
      have to delete. Attributed Checkouts started before the Storefronts came down still count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">20.5</span><b>Sales already made.</b> Creator Rates and Comarket Fees on Qualifying Sales made before the end,
      including their Renewals until each Commission Period ends, stay payable, and we keep billing them monthly. You must keep
      your Connected Stripe Account connected, read-only, until then. If you disconnect it, we may invoice the Renewals we would
      expect from the customer's plan, and you may correct the invoice with your records.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">20.6</span>Sections 7 to 11, 14.4, 15, 17, 18, 20.4 to 20.6 and 21, and Part B, survive the end of this
      agreement.
    </p>

    <h2>21. Working with creators outside Comarket</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">21.1</span>If, within 12 months after we first introduce you to a creator, you pay that creator to promote
      your products outside Comarket, you will tell us and pay us [[NON-CIRCUMVENTION FEE]]. This doesn't apply to a creator who
      was already working with you before we introduced them, if you show that to us.
    </p>

    <h2>22. Other terms</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">22.1</span>This agreement is not exclusive. You may use other marketing channels, and we may work with other
      brands, including your competitors.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">22.2</span>We may change this agreement under section 13 of Part B, and the general terms in Part B apply to
      everything else.
    </p>
